WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.700
One of the interesting by-products of the digitization of everything

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.360
Is the steep rise in nostalgia for physicality

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.500
There's a great lecture from the writer and publisher James Bridle

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.280
Where he laughs about the fact that nobody ever really talked about

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:38.920
The smell of books until e-books started to threaten their existence. All of a sudden we became

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.400
Nostalgic for a quality that we never really noticed before

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:49.610
Or, take the resurgence in vinyl record sales. While it's definitely true that the sound quality of vinyl is amazing

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.650
I'd be willing to bet that our attraction to it

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:57.619
also has something to do with the object itself -- a real thing that takes up space in your home,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.000
a symbol of the analog world.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.080
As more and more of our lives get vacuumed into the digital realm

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.400
We're increasingly likely, I think, to look for these symbols and be attracted to them.  And the crazy thing is that these

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.240
Reminders don't even have to be physical objects.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.300
All they have to do to tap into that nostalgia

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.560
is resemble the traits of those objects.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.520
Think about Instagram filters.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.480
Designed to look like old cameras and film stocks, these filters

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.160
mimic objects that most Instagram users won't ever touch.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.920
As Bridle says, putting a filter on your digital photo is an attempt to codify that image

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:37.420
As memory; to wrap it up in something solid and historical that calls back to a time

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.360
Before everything was fluid and changing and impossible to hold on to.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.129
In this way, our nostalgia becomes an aesthetic,

00:01:45.129 --> 00:01:48.440
and in film, one of the features of that aesthetic is grain.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.540
Film grain is a texture in processed film that has the appearance of tiny particles

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.420
moving around randomly in the image.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.560
The amount of grain has to do with the speed and size of the film stock and the amount of light being used.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.440
During the era when film was the only choice,

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:08.080
many photographers and filmmakers tried hard to eliminate this aspect of film processing.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.879
But it was always a part of cinema, especially in low-budget independent movies using smaller film stocks

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:18.820
Like 16 millimeter, and without access to big professional studio lighting.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.339
These days, it's easy to tap into that indie aesthetic by using grain.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:27.320
The grain itself is loaded with all these associations that reach back into the history of film

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.320
The question is now that grain has its own aesthetic, can that aesthetic be pushed further?

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.000
And that brings me

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.820
to Mandy, the second feature film from Panos Cosmatos

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.209
If you haven't seen it, do that right now, it might still be playing at a local theater near you

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.809
Mandy is a revenge horror movie starring Nicolas Cage

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.579
Reminiscent of the grindhouse films of the 70s and 80s when the movie is set

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.000
It's a simple story as most revenge movies are,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.160
but what sets it apart is the psychoactive vision and focus of Cosmatos,

00:03:00.169 --> 00:03:03.840
who clearly has something very specific in mind visually.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.640
The movie is dark, grimy, often saturated with primary colors

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.071
and completely awash in grain.

00:03:10.071 --> 00:03:10.969
In this way

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:14.479
It's not unlike Cosmatos' first feature, Beyond the Black Rainbow

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.419
Which is much more ambiguous plot wise, but interested in exploring the same visual fetishes

00:03:20.419 --> 00:03:23.389
For Cosmatos, it's not just about paying homage to old films

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.300
It's about learning the expressive potential of existing aesthetics and stretching their qualities

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.200
to see what kinds of feelings and tones they can evoke

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.880
~The way I look at it now though is I feel like in the present, time has no meaning anymore

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:36.620
~Yeah

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.509
~And so it's, I feel like now if choosing an era for your film is almost like choosing a color

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.589
Beyond the Black Rainbow and Mandy invite us to consider film grain as

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:50.449
a visual tool, like lighting or color, not just a byproduct of chemical processing

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:53.600
So what does grain feel like?

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.660
Maybe the most prominent quality of film grain is movement.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:58.008
&lt;The grain is always moving&gt;

00:03:58.008 --> 00:04:02.940
&lt;it's swimming, which means that even in a still life of, let's say a flower on a table&gt;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.100
&lt;that flower is alive even though it's not moving&gt;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.860
Grain gets inside whatever's in the frame and imbues it with motion

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:13.362
In fact, this kind of swimming, breathing movement from within

00:04:13.362 --> 00:04:16.130
mimics the visual effects of hallucinogenic drugs

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.860
Which play a major part in Mandy

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.689
Film grain is hypnotic and that adds to the sense of the film existing in a dreamlike universe

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.310
Or as one horribly bad acid trip

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.760
At times, the grain almost has a paranoid feeling

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.160
like there's something squirming under the image

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.220
Another quality of grain is that it blends

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.500
Grain gives an underlying texture to the frame so that there is a continuity between

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.540
One object and another

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.940
It gives the impression that the image is atomized into tiny particles that could disperse and overlap

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:50.865
Cosmatos often mixes heavy grain with smoke to heighten this effect

00:04:50.865 --> 00:04:52.689
For the characters and the viewers

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.940
Nothing is solid or what it seems

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.714
Things can shift at a moment's notice

00:04:56.714 --> 00:04:58.200
In a world that looks like this

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:01.719
You're always vulnerable, always at risk of coming apart

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:09.860
The most striking example of this is a scene in the first half of Mandy where Mandy herself is kidnapped by a cult and force-fed

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.380
hallucinogenic drugs

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:12.820
Cosmatos turns the dial

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.564
way up on this scene by drenching the image in purple and pink hues

00:05:16.564 --> 00:05:18.800
and adding a trailing effect to movement

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.000
so that when the cult leader

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Jeremiah Sands gesticulates,

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.731
You actually have this steam of grain that falls away from him

00:05:26.731 --> 00:05:31.189
&lt;He graced me despite his heart&gt;

00:05:31.189 --> 00:05:33.000
&lt;of the light&gt;

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:39.380
Then when he kneels down to speak to her up close, Cosmatos actually fades Mandy's face into Sands

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.789
it's hardly noticeable at first and that's

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:42.880
Purposeful

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.240
The texture of the image helps this be so subtle that you're not quite sure if your mind is playing tricks on you

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.852
It's important to note that even though this is cool as hell

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:56.549
and something that film geeks like myself drool over

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.820
This effect is not just visual decoration

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.171
It adds to the story.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:04.370
Sands is trying to assimilate Mandy into his cult,

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:05.320
as you see her face

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:06.620
Fade into his

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:13.920
He's using the qualities of psychoactive drugs to loosen Mandy's grip on her own identity so that he can substitute his own

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.044
Stuff like this is what gives Mandy its power.

00:06:17.044 --> 00:06:20.140
Its aesthetic is not added on to the film after the fact

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.559
It's a film that literally wouldn't

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.360
function if you took those aesthetic features away

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:29.040
That's true for its use of film grain and for all the other

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.140
visual fireworks of the movie

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:35.361
You know as history becomes nostalgia and nostalgia becomes aesthetic,

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:38.729
a great opportunity is being presented to new filmmakers

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.140
I agree with Cosmatos; time has no meaning anymore

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.800
Every aesthetic of the past is a palette for the future

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.820
You can copy and paste those old aesthetics onto your work

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.060
or you can take up the colors with a spirit of experimentation

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:57.300
And have some fucking fun

